     1	# OpenChair
     2	
     3	OpenChair is a handheld Raspberry Pi-based diagnostic reader for powered wheelchairs. It uses non-contact optical sensing to capture the blink pattern of a wheelchair controller's existing diagnostic LED. A separate distance sensor validates that the reader is positioned correctly before a reading is accepted.
     4	
     5	The software will decode supported, verified controller-specific patterns and show an understandable explanation. Unknown or unreliable readings must not produce an invented diagnosis; the device will instead offer retry or manual code entry. The product is designed to operate fully offline.
     6	
     7	> **Status: early development.** No sensor support, diagnostic logic, or real application exists yet. The repository contains project documentation, a working native Linux/WSL2 C23 build and test system, and a minimal scaffold CLI with smoke tests that exist only to exercise that toolchain.
     8	
     9	## Target hardware
    10	
    11	- Raspberry Pi 3 Model B+
    12	- Adafruit ALS-PT19 analog light sensor (optical sensing)
    13	- Adafruit ADS1115 16-bit I²C ADC, address `0x48`, receiving the ALS-PT19 output on channel A0
    14	- Adafruit VL6180X distance/proximity sensor, I²C address `0x29`
    15	- Adafruit PiTFT 2.2" 320x240 HAT Mini Kit (#2315), connected over SPI
    16	- Physical tactile switches for navigation
    17	- 32 GB microSD card
    18	- Regulated 5 V / 2.5 A supply for development and testing
    19	- Rechargeable battery system: planned, but not selected or implemented
    20	
    21	## Operating system and development environment
    22	
    23	- **Deployment target:** Raspberry Pi OS Lite 64-bit is the preferred deployment system, pending actual hardware verification. It has not been finalized or tested on hardware.
    24	- **Current development environment:** WSL2 Ubuntu with GCC 15.2.0 and GNU Make 4.4.1.
    25	- **Language and flags:** the systems core is written in C23 and compiled with `-std=c23 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -pedantic`.
    26	- **Simulation:** software-first development will use simulated sensor inputs until hardware is available. Simulation does not constitute live-hardware verification.
    27	
    28	## Milestone status
    29	
    30	| Milestone | Status |
    31	|-----------|--------|
    32	| M0 | Repository setup verification is complete, subject to closing the remaining documentation and collaboration items. |
    33	| M1 | Project/problem memo received professor approval (5/5). The local memo has since been updated to reflect the current design. |
    34	| M2 | Detailed design and live hardware verification remain to be completed or verified. |
    35	
    36	Mechanisms E (multiprocess fault isolation) and D (custom crash-consistent storage) are provisionally selected, pending Kacper's confirmation and final mapping in `DESIGN.md`.
    37	
    38	## Repository structure
    39	
    40	```
    41	.
    42	├── CLAUDE.md             # team and development rules
    43	├── PROBLEM.md            # problem memo
    44	├── DESIGN.md             # design document (currently empty)
    45	├── Makefile              # native Linux/WSL2 build and test targets (see below)
    46	├── README.md
    47	├── .gitignore
    48	├── src/                  # C23 systems implementation
    49	│   ├── main.c            # scaffold CLI (--version, --help); not the application
    50	│   └── version.c         # scaffold version string
    51	├── include/              # public/shared C headers
    52	│   └── openchair_version.h
    53	├── tests/                # automated tests
    54	│   ├── test_smoke.c      # smoke test of the scaffold CLI
    55	│   └── fixtures/         # simulated/replayed sensor data (empty)
    56	├── YL626/
    57	│   ├── PROMPTLOG.md
    58	│   └── REFLECTION.md
    59	└── kacperm37/
    60	    ├── PROMPTLOG.md
    61	    └── REFLECTION.md
    62	```
    63	
    64	## Build and test
    65	
    66	The build is native to Linux/WSL2 (GCC, GNU Make). There is no cross-compilation or Raspberry Pi deployment yet. The only program is a scaffold CLI (`openchair --version` / `--help`) whose purpose is to give the toolchain something real to build and test. It reads no sensors and produces no diagnoses.
    67	
    68	| Target | Behavior |
    69	|--------|----------|
    70	| `make` / `make all` | Builds `build/plain/openchair`. |
    71	| `make test` | Builds and runs the smoke test (`build/plain/`). It runs the real `openchair` binary and checks its output and exit status. A failing check makes `make test` exit nonzero. |
    72	| `make asan` | Rebuilds in a separate tree (`build/asan/`) with AddressSanitizer + UBSan (UBSan findings are fatal) and runs the same smoke test. |
    73	| `make memcheck` | Runs the plain (non-sanitized) smoke test and its `openchair` children under Valgrind, failing on any error or definite/possible leak. Logs go to `build/valgrind-*.log`. Requires `valgrind` to be installed. |
    74	| `make clean` | Removes `build/`. |
    75	| `make run`, `make soakcheck` | **Not available.** Print a message and exit nonzero; no application or soak test exists. |
    76	| `make deploy` | Does not exist. |
    77	
    78	Plain and sanitizer builds use separate object trees and are never mixed; Valgrind runs only on the plain build because it is incompatible with ASan.
    79	
    80	Verified on WSL2 Ubuntu (GCC 15.2.0, Make 4.4.1, Valgrind 3.26.0): `make test`, `make asan`, and `make memcheck` pass. This is software-only verification. Nothing here has been verified on a Raspberry Pi or with live hardware.

